Adapt Caelestia shell 2.5.0's lock-screen PAM stacks to Guix System.
The password stack includes the system's /etc/pam.d/login instead of calling
pam_faillock and pam_unix directly; pam_faillock cannot record anything when
run unprivileged, and the login stack uses the setuid unix_chkpwd helper.
The optional fprintd and howdy modules are looked up in the system profile.

Upstream status: Not applicable; this is Guix packaging policy.

--- a/assets/pam.d/fprint
+++ b/assets/pam.d/fprint
@@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
 #%PAM-1.0
 
-auth    required    pam_fprintd.so  max-tries=1
+# Guix: PAM loads bare module names only from Linux-PAM's own store directory.
+auth    required    /run/current-system/profile/lib/security/pam_fprintd.so  max-tries=1
--- a/assets/pam.d/howdy
+++ b/assets/pam.d/howdy
@@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
 #%PAM-1.0
 
-auth    required    pam_howdy.so    max-tries=1
+# Guix: PAM loads bare module names only from Linux-PAM's own store directory.
+auth    required    /run/current-system/profile/lib/security/pam_howdy.so    max-tries=1
--- a/assets/pam.d/passwd
+++ b/assets/pam.d/passwd
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #%PAM-1.0
 
-auth    required                    pam_faillock.so     preauth
-auth    [success=1 default=bad]     pam_unix.so         nullok
-auth    [default=die]               pam_faillock.so     authfail
-auth    required                    pam_faillock.so     authsucc
+# Guix: use the auth stack of the system login service (pam_unix through the
+# setuid unix_chkpwd helper).  Upstream's pam_faillock steps would run in the
+# unprivileged shell, which cannot create or write the tally directory, so they
+# would silently record nothing.
+auth    include     /etc/pam.d/login
